== God Takes Notice of the Least of His Saints

The words thus explained, afford unto us two special poins of doctrine.

1\. #emph[Doct.] God taketh notice of every of his saints, even of the least of them, and of kindness showed to them.

Though #emph[Joseph] was but a lad, and sold as a slave, yet being one of the church, one of the little ones, yea #emph[minimorum minimus;] yet in #emph[Aegypt] God takes notice of him, and of that kindness which was showed to him, #emph[Gen.] 32. 5. Many other like examples are noted in the Scripture; but none more fit for our purpose, than the example of #emph[Lazarus,] Luk. 16. 20. Though he were a very poor beggar, and full of sores, and despised of #emph[Dives,] and all his household, yet God took such notice of him, as he made his angels attend him. Yea God took notice of the kindness which the dogs showed him, for their licking of his sores is recorded to all ages. Note those expressions of the #emph[psalmist, Psal. 40. 12. But I am poor and needy, yet the Lord thinketh upon me.] and again #emph[Psal. 34. 6. This poor man cryed, and the Lord heard him, and saved him out of all his troubles.]

Qu. If you ask, why the Lord taketh notice of every of his saints.

Ans. 1. #emph[they are all his children, and of his household;] therefore as a tender, and careful father, and master, he careth for every one. Yea they are all members of the body of Christ. Now the head taketh care of every member, even the least, and taketh notice of every kindness done to any of them.

2\. #emph[they are all by reason of grace in them, as jewels] and #emph[precious stones;] therefore as a provident #emph[Jeweller] he will look to every one of them. Yea that which often appeareth least may be of greater worth; as a little #emph[Diamond] is of more value than a far greater pebble.

This affordeth a singular ground of comfort to such as in this world are desolate, and forsaken: though they be left alone (as #emph[Elijah was] 1 King 19. 10.) and #emph[as a pelican in the wilderness:] or #emph[an owl of the desert,] or #emph[as a sparrow alone upon the house top,] as the psalmist expresseh, #emph[Psal.] 102. 6, 7. Yet is the Lord with them, and takes care of them. Now if the Lord be with us, and taketh care of us, what need me fear?\] how should this encourage us to extend our charity to any one of the saints, and members of Christ, though mean and little in the world, and not able to recompense us: and though none take notice thereof, to consider that the Lord taketh notice, and will abundantly recompense every good work, yea the least kindness we do unto them. Heb. 6. 10. #emph[God is not unrighteous to forget your work, and labour of love, which ye have showed towards his name, in that ye have ministered to the saints, and do minister,] where, #emph[Gods not forgetting their labour of love in ministering to the saints,] doth imply, that as God taketh notice of their beneficence, so he is, and will be ever mindful of such and such persons to support and succour, and every way do them good.

And if God will thus remember those that remember the least of his, how will he take it that they are neglected? Beloved, how is it with Gods little ones at this day? Is it not very low with many, of them? God takes notice how very low it is; and whether you will or no, God will not forsake them; but (that I may use the words of #emph[Mordecai] to #emph[Esther,] with some variation, #emph[Esther.] 4. 14.) if you altogether hold your hand at this time, relief shall arise to them some other way, but you and your house (take heed that word be not verified upon you) shall be destroyed. But who knows whether you are come to your estates for such a time as this!
